1	diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
2	index 748fabc..2e87ffd 100644
3	--- a/README.md
4	+++ b/README.md
5	@@ -10,6 +10,8 @@ Fights happen at sea (§6): J grapples the nearest ship ahead within six tiles.
6	 
7	 The race is for rubbings (§4; the slice plays Sun and Moon, two sealed sources on each island). Each island's rubbings are locked behind its ruin (§3): moored at its dock, press R to read the clue (six carved tablets whose hints give three symbols in order, or a 4 × 4 grid to walk from start to goal) and enter the answer with the keys or clicks. The first solver unlocks the island for everyone, and every solver earns 40 gold once; a wrong answer locks you out for three seconds. Then keep holding E: after six seconds you collect its rubbing of a type you lack (the HUD shows what the island holds, only while you are moored there, and the hold's progress). Win a duel at sea and, within five seconds, press 1 or 2 (or click) to steal one of the loser's types you lack, with 20 % of their gold (the rarest type is taken if you do not choose; a draw moves nothing). The purse beside the game shows your gold, your rubbings and what the last duel took or gave, and whether the winner chose it or the default took it. A player who drops keeps their seat, ship and rubbings for 60 seconds (a fighter away from a duel for ten seconds forfeits it); one who never returns, or quits, leaves a recovery cache at a dock, a chest others see when in sight. Moor there and hold E to claim one rubbing of a type you lack.
8	 
9	+Fruit powers join the race (§9). Four fruit pedestals, two Ember and two Smoke, stand at island docks; a pedestal in sight shows whether it still holds its fruit. Moored at a pedestal's dock, press F to read its kit: the two specials and what they're good for. Hold F for three seconds to eat it. Letting go, casting off, a grapple or a disconnect stops the meal, and if two captains finish together the fruit goes to one of them. The kit replaces your two specials (U, I) in every duel at sea for the rest of the match, even after a defeat. A duel shows both fighters' kits. The arena draws Ember's flame bolts and Smoke's smoke screens, and a fighter inside a screen still shows a startup flash, its position and its health.
10	+
11	 The standalone duel (the combat spike, §18 stage 1) runs beside it: "Create duel" makes a 1v1 duel whose code the second browser enters under "Join duel", and "Practice" fights a server-side dummy (idle or guarding). After a three-second countdown both browsers predict the fight with the shared duel simulation and roll back to the server's inputs (`docs/duel.md`). Keys (§8): WASD or the arrows move (hold back to guard), J light, K heavy, U and I specials, Shift dash, J and K together throw. The HUD shows both health bars, stamina, the round clock, the connection line (round trip, input delay, corrections) and the result; "Show hitboxes" draws hurtboxes and active hitboxes.
12	 
13	 ## Requirements
14	@@ -39,7 +41,7 @@ Set `VITE_SERVER_URL` when the web client should reach a server other than port
15	 
16	 The map is generated from an ASCII sketch of one quarter, `assets/source/maps/sliceSeaSketch.ts`: edit it, then run `npm run map:build -- --from-sketch` (details in `assets/source/maps/README.md`). The `.tmj` stays within the Tiled subset documented in `packages/content/src/tiledMap.ts`, so [Tiled](https://www.mapeditor.org/) opens it.
17	 
18	-`PIRATE_SEA_RACE_TEST_PLACEMENT=1` is a test-only setting, off by default: the dev and test entry point (`npm run dev`, and the server the browser tests start) then serves `POST /__test/place`, which puts a player's ship on a chosen tile. The others, also off by default: `PIRATE_SEA_RACE_TEST_SOURCES` (for example `sun,sun|moon,moon|sun,sun|moon,moon`, each island's source types) deals every match's rubbings in that layout instead of the private shuffle; `PIRATE_SEA_RACE_TEST_TIMINGS` (for example `ready=60,protection=20,match=12000`, in world ticks, 20 a second; also `sectors` and `carriers` for the two timed reveals) shortens the countdown, the departure protection, the match and its reveals; `PIRATE_SEA_RACE_TEST_FINAL_SECRET` (a 32-bit number) pins the secret that picks the final island. `PIRATE_SEA_RACE_TEST_RUINS` (for example `*=open`, or `0=sequence,1=navigation`) opens islands from the start or pins an island's ruin kind. With the placement setting on, `POST /__test/next-match-timings` (for example `{"match": 400}`) also gives the next match created its own timings, and `POST /__test/next-match-ruins` (for example `{"0": "sequence"}`) its own ruins. The production entry (`npm start`) reads none of them.
19	+`PIRATE_SEA_RACE_TEST_PLACEMENT=1` is a test-only setting, off by default: the dev and test entry point (`npm run dev`, and the server the browser tests start) then serves `POST /__test/place`, which puts a player's ship on a chosen tile. The others, also off by default: `PIRATE_SEA_RACE_TEST_SOURCES` (for example `sun,sun|moon,moon|sun,sun|moon,moon`, each island's source types) deals every match's rubbings in that layout instead of the private shuffle; `PIRATE_SEA_RACE_TEST_TIMINGS` (for example `ready=60,protection=20,match=12000`, in world ticks, 20 a second; also `sectors` and `carriers` for the two timed reveals) shortens the countdown, the departure protection, the match and its reveals; `PIRATE_SEA_RACE_TEST_FINAL_SECRET` (a 32-bit number) pins the secret that picks the final island. `PIRATE_SEA_RACE_TEST_RUINS` (for example `*=open`, or `0=sequence,1=navigation`) opens islands from the start or pins an island's ruin kind. `PIRATE_SEA_RACE_TEST_FRUITS` (for example `ember,smoke,smoke,ember`) pins the fruit on each island's pedestal. With the placement setting on, `POST /__test/next-match-timings` (for example `{"match": 400}`) also gives the next match created its own timings, and `POST /__test/next-match-ruins` (for example `{"0": "sequence"}`) its own ruins. The production entry (`npm start`) reads none of them.
20	 
21	 The integration tests run in two groups (`npm run test:integration`): most files in parallel worker processes, with test-only shorter timings, then the netcode harness files (`duelHarness`, `seaDuelHarness`, `devLatency`) one after another on a quiet machine.
